**This run.** baseline 0.092 [0.058, 0.129]; fewshot 0.154 [0.108, 0.200] (+0.063 vs baseline, p = 0.017); pot 0.062 [0.033, 0.096] (-0.029 vs baseline, p = 0.23); n = 240. Wall time per item at batch size 16 on NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090: baseline 0.12 s, fewshot 0.39 s, pot 0.23 s.

**The baseline answered without working.** 240/240 baseline completions are a bare JSON object with nothing before it, although the system message invites the model to work through the arithmetic. The comparison below is therefore direct answer against guided reasoning (fewshot), against code (pot), and later against a fine-tune taught to write a trace. Its 22 accepted items are 11 solved feasible items (6 % of 192 feasible) and 11 trap refusals.

**Why the prompting arms sit below the always-refuse floor of 0.200.** A policy that refuses every item scores the trap share. To beat it a model has to solve feasible items faster than it loses traps.
- `baseline`: refuses 11/46 traps and 41/182 feasible items (p = 0.85); the flag carries no information about infeasibility, so its trap refusals are refusals it would have made anyway. Forcing `feasible = true` on every prediction would score 0.046 against the recorded 0.092; no refused feasible item carried correct values.
- `fewshot`: refuses 9/47 traps and 16/189 feasible items (p = 0.059); the evidence is inconclusive. Forcing `feasible = true` on every prediction would score 0.117 against the recorded 0.154; no refused feasible item carried correct values.
- `pot`: refuses 9/44 traps and 37/162 feasible items (p = 0.84); the flag carries no information about infeasibility, so its trap refusals are refusals it would have made anyway. Forcing `feasible = true` on every prediction would score 0.025 against the recorded 0.062; no refused feasible item carried correct values.

**The few-shot gain is one family copying one example.** Of the 25 items fewshot wins and the baseline loses, 24 are `dilute_stock`, the family of a worked example. Excluding the exemplar families (`dilute_stock`, `well_addressing`; n = 160) fewshot loses: 5 against 10 accepted, exact p = 0.062. The arm is exemplar-sensitive; read the headline delta of +0.063 as a property of these exemplars. The wins are 22 solved feasible items and 3 trap refusals; losses 10.

**PoT's failures sit in the model's programs.** Sandbox outcomes: 174 ok, 42 runtime errors, 24 rejected, 0 timeouts, 0 without code. 24/24 rejected programs hit the token cap, so all of them are truncations rather than policy rejections. 38/42 runtime errors are `true`/`false`/`null` written as JSON inside Python (a dict literal, not a program). PoT ran at 320 new tokens against 512 for the other arms and hit its cap on 30 items; a PoT-below-baseline reading is partly a budget artefact.

**0 of the 3 prompting arms clear the floor.** The next three sections try the weight-level lever.
