**Headline.** baseline 0.092 → sft 0.504 [0.442, 0.567] on n = 240 items; delta +0.412 [0.342, 0.483], McNemar p < 0.001. Arms above the always-refuse floor (0.200): `sft`.

**SFT learned the trained procedures and transferred nothing.** In the trained families SFT solves 100/128 feasible items where the baseline solved 11. On the 80 held-out items no arm, SFT included, solves a single feasible item (0/64). Held-out trap refusals: baseline 6/16, fewshot 5/16, pot 3/16, sft 5/16. The always-refuse policy scores 0.200 on the held-out items; every arm sits below it there. SFT emits exactly the schema's keys on 80/80 held-out items, so the failure is content, not format. The held-out design did its job: it separated procedure from template, and the answer on this run was template.

**SFT's `feasible` flag:** refuses 21/48 traps against 33/192 feasible items (p < 0.001); the flag carries information: it refuses traps more often than feasible items.

**Paired against the baseline**, SFT wins 108 items and loses 9 (p < 0.001); wins by family: serial_dilution 32, master_mix 29, dilute_stock 28, well_addressing 17, normalize_samples 2; losses by family: serial_dilution 4, normalize_samples 3, dilute_stock 1, well_addressing 1; held-out wins: 2 (17 of all wins are trap refusals).

